WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:02.210
- When you're sitting in a math class

00:00:02.210 --> 00:00:05.480
and the teacher starts writing
some symbols on the board

00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:08.610
that you might not quite
understand just yet,

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:09.797
it might be tempting to say,

00:00:09.797 --> 00:00:12.207
"Hey, why do I need to lean this?

00:00:12.207 --> 00:00:14.980
"This seems a little bit abstract for me."

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:16.070
And to answer that,

00:00:16.070 --> 00:00:19.680
I'll tell you a story that one
of my early advisors told me

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:21.210
when I was just starting Khan Academy.

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:23.580
This is someone who doesn't
come from engineering,

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:25.140
doesn't come from science,

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:27.090
and doesn't come from mathematics.

00:00:27.090 --> 00:00:29.330
He's actually a major restaurateur who has

00:00:29.330 --> 00:00:32.160
a chain of restaurants some
of you might have gone to.

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:35.730
And he says when he hires people,

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:38.180
he thinks about whether they
understand algebra or not.

00:00:38.180 --> 00:00:39.020
In fact, in his mind,

00:00:39.020 --> 00:00:41.830
he devices the world into
people who understand algebra

00:00:41.830 --> 00:00:43.620
and people who do not understand algebra.

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:47.490
And the reason why he told
me he does that is he says

00:00:47.490 --> 00:00:49.430
when people understand algebra,

00:00:49.430 --> 00:00:52.560
it gives him confidence that
they have the critical thinking

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:54.450
and analytical skills to tackle

00:00:54.450 --> 00:00:57.840
pretty much any problem in any domain.

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:00.240
And I think that is the practical reason

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:01.910
why algebra is so powerful.

00:01:01.910 --> 00:01:04.520
It's really going to start to fine-tune

00:01:04.520 --> 00:01:06.960
your critical thinking
skills and your logic.

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:09.580
And beyond that, it's
frankly just beautiful.

00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:12.330
A lot of the equations
you're going to learn,

00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:14.100
the ways of manipulating equations,

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:17.210
philosophers, mathematicians
were developing these

00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:18.920
for thousands of years,

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:21.830
and now you get to understand the outcome

00:01:21.830 --> 00:01:23.540
of their thousands of years

00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:24.720
of hitting a head against the wall

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:27.300
and coming up with these
beautiful expressions

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:31.270
that are really the fundamental
truths of the universe.

00:01:31.270 --> 00:01:33.940
So when you go into this algebra journey

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:35.120
that we're about to start,

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:36.400
learn the math,

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:37.850
learn the tools you're about to,

00:01:37.850 --> 00:01:40.000
because it's really a form of expression.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:43.600
It's a form, it's a way of representing

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:47.410
pure ideas in the universe and our minds.

00:01:47.410 --> 00:01:48.720
In these lessons,

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:50.470
we're going to talk about
some of that history,

00:01:50.470 --> 00:01:52.300
the origins of algebra.

00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:53.600
We're also going to think about

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:55.810
what is an algebraic expression,

00:01:55.810 --> 00:01:58.320
and what are things like
unknowns and variables?

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:00.390
And finally, we're going to really start

00:02:00.390 --> 00:02:01.730
to build out our toolkit

00:02:01.730 --> 00:02:03.690
on how do we manipulate these things?

00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:05.840
How do we speak the language of algebra

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:10.000
and utilize these tools to
solve everyday problems?

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:11.940
So let's start this adventure together.

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:13.660
There might be moments where you're

00:02:13.660 --> 00:02:15.430
a little bit unsure of yourself,

00:02:15.430 --> 00:02:17.160
or you're a little bit not sure

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:18.930
of how one thing leads to another.

00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:20.120
But trust me,

00:02:20.120 --> 00:02:23.330
if you go into the content, pause videos,

00:02:23.330 --> 00:02:25.110
and see if you can
solve things on your own

00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:27.230
before I work through it with you,

00:02:27.230 --> 00:02:29.580
and then make sure again that
you can do it on your own.

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:32.450
And I encourage you to
go to khanacademy.org

00:02:32.450 --> 00:02:35.410
and practice these same
concepts on the site,

00:02:35.410 --> 00:02:37.000
where you'll get immediate feedback,

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:39.370
to make sure that you're
understanding them.

00:02:39.370 --> 00:02:41.730
Algebra is going to be a powerful toolkit

00:02:41.730 --> 00:02:43.050
not just for your studies,

00:02:43.050 --> 00:02:44.653
but for your entire life.

